WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.399 --> 00:00:05.950
Not all filmmakers like their movies to have
simple endings. Some leave their last scenes

00:00:05.950 --> 00:00:12.139
ambiguous to keep audiences thinking. But
sometimes that plan backfires, resulting in

00:00:12.139 --> 00:00:16.900
endings that are just plain confusing. Beware
of spoilers...

00:00:16.900 --> 00:00:18.070
Inception

00:00:18.070 --> 00:00:23.619
Christopher Nolan's 2010 film left audiences'
minds spinning as much as the top in the final

00:00:23.619 --> 00:00:27.999
shot. Just when it looks like the top is about
to spin out and tumble, the screen cuts to

00:00:27.999 --> 00:00:33.930
black. The final shot shows Dom Cobb reuniting
with his kids. But we never know if it's really

00:00:33.930 --> 00:00:39.320
happening or if it's a dream. Fans debated
the scene endlessly for years after Inception

00:00:39.320 --> 00:00:44.250
came out...but according to Nolan, the non-ending
is actually kind of the whole point.

00:00:44.250 --> 00:00:49.350
In 2015, the director gave the commencement
speech at Princeton University, and told the

00:00:49.350 --> 00:00:55.210
grads to "chase their reality." He used the
ending of Inception as an example, saying:

00:00:55.210 --> 00:01:00.880
"[Cobb] was off with his kids, he was in his
own subjective reality. He didn't really care

00:01:00.880 --> 00:01:06.390
anymore, and that makes a statement: perhaps,
all levels of reality are valid. The camera

00:01:06.390 --> 00:01:11.190
moves over the spinning top just before it
appears to be wobbling, it was cut to black."

00:01:11.190 --> 00:01:16.520
In short, the ending of the movie is up to
us — and we're right either way.

00:01:16.520 --> 00:01:18.310
The Dark Knight Rises

00:01:18.310 --> 00:01:22.750
The ending to Nolan's Dark Knight trilogy
isn't quite as vague as Inception, but it

00:01:22.750 --> 00:01:27.520
still left viewers debating after the credits
started rolling. After flying a nuclear bomb

00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:33.850
out of Gotham City, Batman escapes the blast...off-screen.
We know this because, later, while Alfred

00:01:33.850 --> 00:01:38.810
is in Florence, he sees Bruce Wayne sitting
at a table, enjoying a meal with ex-Catwoman

00:01:38.810 --> 00:01:44.260
Selina Kyle. Some fans have theorized that
this is all a dream — that Batman actually

00:01:44.260 --> 00:01:50.120
died in the explosion, and that Alfred simply
imagined seeing his friend taking in the Italian

00:01:50.120 --> 00:01:51.120
sunshine.

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:56.240
But that's bat-baloney. Before the movie's
end, we learn along with Lucius Fox that Wayne

00:01:56.240 --> 00:02:01.380
fixed the Bat-plane's autopilot six months
before the final showdown in Gotham. That's

00:02:01.380 --> 00:02:06.060
all the exposition necessary for viewers to
know that Batman jumped out while the plane

00:02:06.060 --> 00:02:07.640
flies the bomb toward the bay.

00:02:07.640 --> 00:02:12.319
And sure, when Alfred sees Wayne in Florence,
it's exactly how Alfred describes it earlier

00:02:12.319 --> 00:02:17.040
in the film. But that's not a dream — it's
just the best way for Wayne to show Alfred

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:22.139
he's alive. Moreover, Selina Kyle is there,
wearing Wayne's mother's necklace, which she

00:02:22.139 --> 00:02:26.609
steals at the beginning of the movie. Alfred
doesn't know she and Wayne have become an

00:02:26.609 --> 00:02:31.129
item, and he'd already quit working for Bruce
before Batman and Catwoman teamed up to save

00:02:31.129 --> 00:02:32.239
Gotham City.

00:02:32.239 --> 00:02:36.879
As if all that isn't enough, Christian Bale
himself thinks that Bruce is alive by the

00:02:36.879 --> 00:02:41.310
end of the movie. He explained during an interview
while promoting Exodus: Gods and Kings:

00:02:41.310 --> 00:02:46.409
"He was just content with me being alive and
left because that was always the life that

00:02:46.409 --> 00:02:51.980
he wanted for him. And I find it very interesting
and with most films, I tend to say ‘It’s

00:02:51.980 --> 00:02:55.999
what the audience thinks it is.’ My personal
opinion? No, it was not a dream. That was

00:02:55.999 --> 00:03:03.400
for real and he was just delighted that finally
he had freed himself from the privilege, but

00:03:03.400 --> 00:03:05.260
ultimately the burden of being Bruce Wayne."

00:03:05.260 --> 00:03:10.300
None of this matters anyway. Batfleck is the
wave of the future! But let's shift our gaze

00:03:10.300 --> 00:03:14.530
toward the ghost of Batman's past...

00:03:14.530 --> 00:03:15.530
Birdman

00:03:15.530 --> 00:03:21.379
Alejandro G. Iñárritu's film about a washed-up
actor trying to make a comeback on Broadway

00:03:21.379 --> 00:03:26.590
has the kind of weird ending that puts Inception
to shame. Throughout the film, Riggan Thomas

00:03:26.590 --> 00:03:31.459
Thomson is shown as having superpowers, only
to have them later be explained as being all

00:03:31.459 --> 00:03:36.379
in his head. In the final scene, Riggan's
daughter Sam enters his hospital room to find

00:03:36.379 --> 00:03:41.430
his bed empty and the window open. Sirens
and voices can be heard coming from the street

00:03:41.430 --> 00:03:47.629
below. Initially, Sam looks down, but she
slowly turns her head up to the sky and she

00:03:47.629 --> 00:03:53.750
smiles. Some might think this means Riggan
actually does have powers, and has flown away.

00:03:53.750 --> 00:03:58.449
But...probably not. What really seems to have
happened is that Riggan has successfully committed

00:03:58.449 --> 00:04:04.309
suicide, which he failed to do on the previous
day. Sam, for her part, seems to start hallucinating

00:04:04.309 --> 00:04:09.059
just like her dad. The fact that she has bird
tattoos on her arm and that her father played

00:04:09.059 --> 00:04:13.990
a superhero with bird-based powers suggests
the strong connection between the two. Sam

00:04:13.990 --> 00:04:19.959
seems to leave the real world to enter a fantasy
where her father is still alive, soaring above

00:04:19.959 --> 00:04:26.020
the clouds. The film is subtitled The Unexpected
Virtue of Ignorance, after all. Here, Sam

00:04:26.020 --> 00:04:28.360
chooses to ignore reality.

00:04:28.360 --> 00:04:33.300
One of Birdman's four screenwriters, Alexander
Dinelaris Jr., hinted during an interview

00:04:33.300 --> 00:04:39.210
with HuffPost Live that the key to their understanding
of the ending lies within Sam's relationship

00:04:39.210 --> 00:04:40.210
with her father:

00:04:40.210 --> 00:04:44.400
"I think when we found the relationship with
the daughter, we started to understand what

00:04:44.400 --> 00:04:49.400
Riggan’s story was. Once she got down, Emma’s
big monologue, in the basement, we started

00:04:49.400 --> 00:04:54.400
to understand the relationship and what it
was. We’re not going to sit around and explain

00:04:54.400 --> 00:04:55.430
the ending."

00:04:55.430 --> 00:04:56.430
Vanilla Sky

00:04:56.430 --> 00:05:03.419
Instead of a feelgood romance or a fun coming-of-age
story, viewers of Vanilla Sky got a moody,

00:05:03.419 --> 00:05:09.960
elliptical remake of a hit Spanish film. A
feckless playboy suffers near-death and disfigurement

00:05:09.960 --> 00:05:15.550
after a relationship with his new girlfriend
plunges his ex into homicidal obsession. But

00:05:15.550 --> 00:05:20.349
once his face is surgically restored, his
life really starts to go haywire.

00:05:20.349 --> 00:05:25.370
Viewers are told that much of what they've
seen is a lucid dream in Cruise's brain, which

00:05:25.370 --> 00:05:30.509
has been held in a cryogenic stasis for more
than 100 years. The more troubling elements

00:05:30.509 --> 00:05:35.129
of the narrative are apparently the result
of a glitch. He's given the choice to either

00:05:35.129 --> 00:05:40.069
reboot the dream or exit it once and for all
by jumping off a building and being brought

00:05:40.069 --> 00:05:45.270
back to life. He jumps — and the last shot
is of the protagonist opening his eyes, starting

00:05:45.270 --> 00:05:47.110
his life over again.

00:05:47.110 --> 00:05:51.479
Director Cameron Crowe said that the correct
answer to understanding the movie might just

00:05:51.479 --> 00:05:56.250
be accepting what you see onscreen as the
actual events of the story, according to his

00:05:56.250 --> 00:06:00.879
comments on the DVD's commentary track. Crowe
seemed to lean in that direction while talking

00:06:00.879 --> 00:06:05.439
with Film School Rejects about the film's
unused alternate ending as well, saying:

00:06:05.439 --> 00:06:09.920
"You want people to understand what you're
going for, so the question is, looking at

00:06:09.920 --> 00:06:15.319
both endings: Did the pendulum swing too much
in the direction of us explaining stuff? I

00:06:15.319 --> 00:06:20.139
think it did. The original ending was more
open-ended, a little less explained."

00:06:20.139 --> 00:06:21.310
Barton Fink

00:06:21.310 --> 00:06:26.599
At the end of this Coen Brothers flick, tortured
writer Barton Fink wanders onto a beach, where

00:06:26.599 --> 00:06:31.319
he meets a woman resembling the picture decorating
his sparse, depressing hotel room. Shortly

00:06:31.319 --> 00:06:35.439
after they meet, the movie abruptly ends,
potentially leaving some viewers scratching

00:06:35.439 --> 00:06:38.099
their heads. What's it mean? Here's one way
to look at it.

00:06:38.099 --> 00:06:43.949
The picture represents the idea of Hollywood.
It's a place of fantasy, beaches, and beautiful

00:06:43.949 --> 00:06:49.030
women. Meanwhile, throughout the entire film,
Fink is subjected to the reality of Hollywood.

00:06:49.030 --> 00:06:55.060
He's had his script torn apart by an executive.
He found out his hero, writer W. P. Mayhew

00:06:55.060 --> 00:06:59.949
is a washed-up alcoholic, and that Mayhew's
wife writes his novels for him. And he's fled

00:06:59.949 --> 00:07:04.039
from both a burning hotel and a shotgun-wielding
maniac.

00:07:04.039 --> 00:07:06.520
“Ahhhh! Ahhhh!”

00:07:06.520 --> 00:07:09.979
You'd think that finally finding the woman
on the beach would mean that Fink is at the

00:07:09.979 --> 00:07:15.810
end of his trials, having reached his reward
and a place where he feels safe. But in fact,

00:07:15.810 --> 00:07:19.660
he's learned the truth about the dangerous
world in which he now exists.

00:07:19.660 --> 00:07:21.930
Joel Coen explained in a 1991 interview:

00:07:21.930 --> 00:07:26.500
"Some people have suggested that the whole
second part of the film is nothing but a nightmare.

00:07:26.500 --> 00:07:31.480
But it was never our intention to, in any
literal sense, depict some bad dream, and

00:07:31.480 --> 00:07:36.870
yet it is true that we were aiming for a logic
of the irrational. We wanted the film's atmosphere

00:07:36.870 --> 00:07:39.919
to reflect the psychological state of the
protagonist."

00:07:39.919 --> 00:07:44.650
That wouldn't be the last time the ending
of a Coen brothers movie left audiences debating

00:07:44.650 --> 00:07:46.990
after leaving the theater...

00:07:46.990 --> 00:07:48.050
No Country For Old Men

00:07:48.050 --> 00:07:53.690
At the end of this blood-soaked neo-Western,
Sheriff Ed Tom Bell tells his wife about two

00:07:53.690 --> 00:07:58.279
dreams he has about his father. In the first
dream, he loses some money his father gave

00:07:58.279 --> 00:08:03.599
him. In the second dream, Bell sees his father
holding a torch, riding ahead into the darkness

00:08:03.599 --> 00:08:05.319
of a snowy mountain pass.

00:08:05.319 --> 00:08:09.650
Shortly before Bell tells the stories of his
dreams, he tells his wife that his father

00:08:09.650 --> 00:08:15.689
died young. That, in a sense, his father will
always be a younger man. More importantly,

00:08:15.689 --> 00:08:20.129
throughout the movie, Bell ponders the violence
in the area where he's sheriff. And since

00:08:20.129 --> 00:08:25.669
he's close to retirement, he wonders whether
he's too old for the world. The title of the

00:08:25.669 --> 00:08:31.599
movie is No Country For Old Men, and Bell
is one of them. It's become too violent too

00:08:31.599 --> 00:08:36.800
quickly for someone of his age, and he can
no longer cope. The world needs someone younger,

00:08:36.800 --> 00:08:42.050
like his father, to light the way in the ever-growing
darkness around it — exactly like the second

00:08:42.050 --> 00:08:43.140
dream Bell describes.

00:08:43.140 --> 00:08:47.120
As for the first dream? Maybe Bell just needs
a new wallet.

00:08:47.120 --> 00:08:48.320
Mulholland Drive

00:08:48.320 --> 00:08:52.950
David Lynch fans don't watch his work for
straightforward narratives. But even in the

00:08:52.950 --> 00:08:58.960
context of his endearingly weird filmography,
2001's Mulholland Drive is tough to figure

00:08:58.960 --> 00:09:04.070
out. Lynch himself has steadfastly refused
to help untangle the movie, which moves in

00:09:04.070 --> 00:09:09.660
jittery circles around an actress, a mysterious
woman, and a film director…all of whom are

00:09:09.660 --> 00:09:14.030
mixed up in a dreamlike and frequently nonsensical
series of events.

00:09:14.030 --> 00:09:18.490
Ultimately, the film's ending is every bit
as open to interpretation as the rest of the

00:09:18.490 --> 00:09:23.270
film — and although viewers are welcome
to delve into the many theories attempting

00:09:23.270 --> 00:09:28.070
to explain what Lynch might have meant, the
best explanation was arguably posed by the

00:09:28.070 --> 00:09:31.470
late film critic Roger Ebert. According to
him,

00:09:31.470 --> 00:09:36.660
"The movie is hypnotic; we're drawn along
as if one thing leads to another but nothing

00:09:36.660 --> 00:09:41.620
leads anywhere, and that's even before the
characters start to fracture and recombine

00:09:41.620 --> 00:09:46.800
like flesh caught in a kaleidoscope. There
is no explanation. There may not even be a

00:09:46.800 --> 00:09:47.800
mystery."

00:09:47.800 --> 00:09:52.560
So what's the explanation here? Uh...pretty
much whatever you want.

00:09:52.560 --> 00:09:53.800
“You’re welcome!”

00:09:53.800 --> 00:09:58.110
Thanks for watching! Click the Looper icon
to subscribe to our YouTube channel. Plus

00:09:58.110 --> 00:10:00.560
check out all this other cool stuff we know
you'll love too.

